From 8701ae1a0bce0ae97ead63eb86ff950ca49ba3f6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 13:01:33 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08058/11362] [outer-loop] security audit fix 2/5: close the
 sandbox __import__ bypass

`run_in_sandbox` executed hypothesis code with the real `__builtins__`, while
`check_imports` only walked `ast.Import` / `ast.ImportFrom` nodes. Anything that
avoided the literal word `import` therefore ignored the blocklist entirely. The
audit PoC ran a real shell command:

    import os                      -> blocked  (control)
    __import__('os').getcwd()      -> SUCCEEDED, returned cwd
    __import__('subprocess').run() -> SUCCEEDED, executed `echo`

Fix: hypothesis code now gets a restricted `__builtins__` COPY carrying a
guarded `__import__` that enforces the same blocklist at call time, so the
statement form and the dynamic form route through one check. `__import__` is
replaced rather than removed because `import jax` compiles to a `__import__`
call -- deleting it would break every legitimate hypothesis. `open`, `eval`,
`exec`, `compile`, `input`, `breakpoint` and `help` are removed outright.

`builtins`/`sys`/`gc`/`inspect` are blocked unconditionally, NOT via
BLOCKED_MODULES: that constant is public API and a caller narrowing
`SandboxConfig.blocked_modules` must not be able to re-open
`builtins.__import__` or `sys.modules['os']`.

Also corrects the module docstring, which advertised "Import whitelist
enforcement" -- a claim the implementation did not honour. It now states
plainly that this is a guardrail against accidental misuse, not a security
boundary, and that gVisor (CARNOT_USE_SANDBOX=1) is the real containment.

That scope note is not hedging: a reflective escape via
`object.__subclasses__()` was verified STILL OPEN after this fix, reaching
`os.getcwd()` without ever calling `__import__`. It is written into the
docstring as a worked counterexample and asserted by
`test_reflective_escape_is_still_open`, so the honest scope claim cannot rot
into a false one -- if CPython ever closes it, that test fails and the doc gets
revisited.

16 new regression tests (every PoC above, plus the legitimate-import and
host-builtins-isolation cases). 29 pass with the existing sandbox suite.
Mutation check: no tracked file rewritten by the run.
